# PART 3: Final Comparison – Java vs Web

| Feature | Maven Java | Maven Web |
|---|---|---|
| Build job | MavenJava_Build | MavenWeb_Build |
| Test job | MavenJava_Test | MavenWeb_Test |
| Deploy job | Not required | MavenWeb_Deploy |
| GitHub | Java repository | Web repository |
| Build command | `clean`, `install` | `clean`, `install` |
| Test command | `test` | `test` |
| Artifact | JAR | WAR |
| Archive | `target/*.jar` | `target/*.war` |
| Deployment | Not required | Tomcat |
| Pipeline view | Optional | MavenWeb_Pipeline |

# PART 4: Important Jenkins Concepts for Viva

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Jenkins | Automation server used for CI/CD. |
| Maven | Build automation tool for Java projects. |
| Freestyle Project | A Jenkins job configured through the user interface. |
| GitHub | Hosts the project source code. |
| Source Code Management | Fetches source code from GitHub. |
| Build Step | Executes commands to build or test a project. |
| `mvn clean` | Removes previous generated build files. |
| `mvn install` | Builds and installs the artifact into the local Maven repository. |
| `mvn test` | Executes test cases. |
| JAR | Java Archive file. |
| WAR | Web Application Archive file. |
| Archive Artifacts | Stores generated files in Jenkins. |
| Copy Artifacts | Copies files from one Jenkins job to another. |
| Post-build Action | Executes an action after a job finishes. |
| Tomcat | Server used to run Java web applications. |
| Context Path | URL path used to access a deployed application. |
| Pipeline View | Displays the sequence of connected Jenkins jobs. |

## Final revision – Commands and job names

**Java:**

```text
MavenJava_Build
    clean
    install
    Archive target/*.jar
        |
        v
MavenJava_Test
    test
```

**Web:**

```text
MavenWeb_Build
    clean
    install
    Archive target/*.war
        |
        v
MavenWeb_Test
    test
    Archive target/*.war
        |
        v
MavenWeb_Deploy
    Copy WAR
    Deploy to Tomcat
```

**Remember these three things for your practical exam:**

1. Java project generates a **JAR** file.
2. Web project generates a **WAR** file.
3. Web deployment uses **Tomcat** to run the application.

**One important Maven detail:** `mvn install` already runs the test phase as part of its lifecycle. If your lab requires a separate test job, that job should check out the source code and execute `mvn test` separately.
